# Commercial ROM images
# `roms/` covers the directory; the bare `roms` covers a symlink or junction to
# it, which git reports as an untracked *file* and the directory pattern misses.
roms/
roms
*.gba
*.gb
*.gbc
*.sav
*.srm
*.mss
*.ss?
*.state

# Extracted/generated game data
extracted/
dump/
dumps/
stock/
exports/
*.bin
*.dump
*.raw

# Extracted graphics. Project extraction writes one indexed PNG
# per sprite payload; those pixels are the publisher's artwork, so they are
# project OUTPUT and are never committed, wherever a project happens to sit.
graphics/assets/
**/graphics/assets/

# Generated builds
build/
dist/
node_modules/
.astro/
*.egg-info/
*.spec

# Release artefacts. A distributable patch and a frozen executable are both
# build output, so neither can be committed by accident; `docs/release-artefacts.md`
# states what a user distributes and what must never travel with it.
*.bps
*.exe

# The generated build-identity record a package build bakes in, so a packaged
# application can name the commit it was cut from. It is a module rather than a
# data file so it travels inside a frozen build through the import graph, and it
# is never tracked because it names one machine's commit rather than the
# project's content.
tools/webfoot/_build_identity.py

# Generated ROM provenance manifests. A build or an authored-image save writes
# one BESIDE its output image (`authored_image.manifest_path_for`), and `*.gba`
# does not match `foo.gba.dragonbyte.json` - so a derivative saved outside an
# ignored build directory would otherwise commit the manifest, which carries
# ROM-derived provenance, while gitignoring the ROM it describes.
*.dragonbyte.json

# External upstream repositories
extern/

# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.hypothesis/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Local environment secrets. Tooling credentials (an API key for a local
# service, for example) live in a local .env read by the shell or by the tool
# itself; nothing in this
# repository loads it at runtime, so it is local configuration, never project
# content. `.env.example` is deliberately NOT ignored: a template carrying only
# variable NAMES is the trackable way to document what a local .env needs.
#
# The scope is the conventional `.env` / `.env.local` / `.env.<mode>.local`
# delimiter set, not every name containing "env". Two variants are therefore
# still stageable, named here so the gap stays deliberate rather than assumed:
# `.envrc` (direnv) and `.env.<mode>` with no `.local` suffix, such as
# `.env.production`. A `.local` suffix always means local configuration, so
# `.env.example.local` is ignored as well.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# Editor and local tool state. Anything a tool writes into the checkout for its
# own use belongs here rather than in version control; the specific directories
# a given tool uses are listed in .git/info/exclude so this file stays generic.
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local research scratch space. Ad-hoc probes and one-off scripts live here
# while a change is in flight; they are not deliverables and are never
# committed.
/scratchpad/
